  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A solar panel turns sunlight directly into electricity for your home. The working part is not the glass cover. It is the thin solar cell underneath. When sunlight enters the solar cell, photons transfer energy to electrons in the silicon. That energy frees some electrons, leaving behind positively charged vacancies called holes. A built-in electric field inside the solar cell pushes the freed electrons in one direction. Metal contacts collect those electrons and send their flow through an external circuit as direct current. The moving electrons can power a load before returning to the solar cell. For many home appliances, an inverter changes the solar panel's direct current into alternating current. So the solar panel supplies electricity because sunlight frees charges and the cell guides their flow.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9788733031600714,
  "low_confidence_words": [],
